#usda 1.0
(
    doc = """A small pick-and-place work cell for the botrail demo: the floor
and its markings, a pedestal-mounted robot, a pallet build station, a control
cabinet, and the goods and taught poses of the line. Z-up, meters.

This layer is the *layout* — where things stand and what a motion is taught
to. The standard products in the cell are not modelled here: the belt, the
rack and the guarding are ordered from the model catalog in demo.py, so
their sizes are ones that are sold and every one of them lands on the bill
of materials with a part number.

Colours are `primvars:displayColor`, which USD defines in *linear* space, so
the numbers here are darker than the sRGB swatch they represent (concrete
#6b6e73 is 0.147/0.156/0.171, not 0.42/0.43/0.45). botrail inherits a
constant displayColor down the namespace, so a group Xform paints its whole
subtree and only the parts that differ restate it.

Anything the demo depends on is load-bearing and should not be moved:
  * MountFrame at z=0.39+0.01 — the pedestal top with the base clearance
  * Conveyor/PickFrame + Box_A at belt top 0.55 + box half 0.03
  * Pallet/PlaceFrame over Crate_2's lid at 0.42
  * Pallet and PalletFar stand clear of their pedestal's base plate (near
    edge at x = -0.26 against the plate's -0.23) — move one back in and the
    riser goes through the boards, which no collision check will tell you
  * nothing but the cartons may have its origin inside the conveyor's
    transport zone (the whole belt: x -2.35..1.45, y 0.42..0.82,
    z 0.55..0.70), or the belt would carry it along with the goods —
    which is why the photo-eye bodies sit at y = 0.32 / 0.92, outside it
"""
    defaultPrim = "World"
    metersPerUnit = 1
    upAxis = "Z"
)

def Xform "World"
{
